Vitreous Stomp
Ingredients: CHICAGO / CITY / URBAN BLUES + HYPNOTIC, TRANCE-LIKE & MINIMAL + Whistle
Creative Catalyst: Born among nomadic desert caravaners who haul and repair salvaged synthesizers over open glassblowing hearths, trading patched circuitry for water along abandoned freight corridors.

Vitreous Stomp is the raw, heavy friction of Chicago bottleneck blues ripped away from urban taverns and stretched into an austere, sand-blasted desert trance. Instead of shifting through traditional twelve-bar progressions, single modal blues riffs are hammered down into hypnotic, cyclically repeating loops that refuse to resolve. Below these grinding, heat-warped guitars, salvaged Japanese and American analog synthesizers throb on sagging solar batteries, while high, piercing whistles—both human lips and cracked ceramic pipes—cut through the heavy low-end mud like spectral switchyard signals across an empty salt basin.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 72–82 BPM. A slow, heavy, locomotive stomp driven by an unswerving heel-drop downbeat on resonant wood-and-tin scrap boxes, locked against drifting, syncopated clock dividers from salvaged synth sequencers.
- Harmonic Palette: Stark Dorian and Mixolydian modes laced with microtonal blues curls (bent minor thirds, flatted fifths); guitars tune down to low open-D or open-G drone tunings calibrated to match the thermal drift of sun-baked circuit boards.
- Production Signatures: 
  - Vitreous Crucible Induction: Running electric lap-steel and whistle lines through contact transducers clamped to cooling quartz glass flasks, capturing the micro-fracturing and thermal shrinkage of molten silica as acoustic tape-crackle and physical compression.
  - Caliche-Buried Spring Reverbs: Long-tank spring reverb units buried twelve inches beneath arid desert soil to radically damp high frequencies, transforming metallic splashes into a choked, subterranean thud.
  - Voltage-Starved Oscillator Sag: Monosynths powered by dying solar cells, causing sub-bass square waves to choke, stutter, and growl whenever the player kicks the master stompbox.
- Dynamics: Monolithic, cyclical accumulation. Tracks rarely drop or break; instead, they establish a single suffocating groove and slowly bleed in heat, tape distortion, and screaming overblown harmonics until the listener enters a trance state, ending only when the power is abruptly cut.

Scene & Origin Lore
The genre ignited along the dry basin routes connecting decommissioned midwestern industrial depots to the southern badlands. Nomadic convoy crews known as the Solder-Caravans bartered salvaged electronics—discarded polyphonic circuit boards, rusted patch bays, broken tube organs—transporting them on flatbed trucks that carried their own portable glassmaking kilns. The caravaners dressed for wind and heat: frayed workwear jackets lined with silver mylar insulation blankets, green-tinted arc-welder goggles, and fingerless mechanic gloves tipped with slide-rings cut from discarded radio vacuum tubes. At dusk, when the caravans circled, traders gathered around the glow of the annealing pits. Here, disputes over salvaged components, territory, and fuel were settled through "blow-downs"—hypnotic, sweat-drenched musical standoffs where slide players, whistling signalers, and synthesizer operators locked into one unyielding groove for hours, testing whose battery rig and stamina would survive the night.

Typical Instruments: Overdriven resonator guitars and lap steels with copper-pipe slides, salvaged analog monosynths (Korg MS-20, Roland SH-101, ARP Axxe), overblown metal slide whistles and breathy human lip whistling amplified through Green Bullet harmonica microphones, foot-stomped tin ammo crates filled with desert gravel, and open-reel magnetic tape delays exposed to grit.

For Fans Of: Junior Kimbrough, Suicide, Tinariwen, Silver Apples

